-- Q3391: Advanced Lease Transactions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The seller-lessee recognizing an immediate loss on the sale","The buyer-lessor recording additional goodwill for the discount","The seller-lessee recording the shortfall as a prepaid lease asset","No adjustment since the transaction is recorded at the sale price"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 842-40-30, when the sale price is below fair value in a sale-leaseback, the difference is treated as a prepayment of rent by the seller-lessee. The below-market leaseback payments compensate for the below-market sale price. Wrong (A): A loss is not recognized because the shortfall represents an advance payment for below-market future rent. Wrong (B): The buyer-lessor does not record goodwill; the below-market purchase is offset by below-market lease receipts. Wrong (D): ASC 842 requires adjustments when sale-leaseback terms are off-market to reflect the economic substance.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3391;

-- Q3399: Pension and Postretirement Benefits [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Continue reporting the PBO on its balance sheet going forward","Transfer the remaining obligation to a qualified successor plan","Recognize all unrecognized prior service costs and net losses immediately","Report the settlement disclosure as a subsequent event footnote only"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 715-30-35-80, when a defined benefit plan is terminated and all obligations are settled through annuity purchases or lump-sum payments, all previously unrecognized amounts in AOCI (prior service costs, actuarial gains/losses) must be recognized immediately in earnings. Wrong (A): Once all obligations are settled, the PBO is eliminated; no further balance sheet reporting is required. Wrong (B): Transferring to a successor plan is not settlement; settlement requires irrevocable discharge of the obligation. Wrong (D): Plan termination with full settlement is a recognized event requiring journal entries, not merely a footnote disclosure.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3399;

-- Q3400: Pension and Postretirement Benefits [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Incorporates projected future salary increases into the calculation","Always exceeds the projected benefit obligation in total amount","Reflects only benefits employees can receive regardless of continued service","Applies exclusively to defined contribution plan obligations"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 715, the vested benefit obligation (VBO) includes only benefits that employees have earned the right to receive even if they leave the company. It is a subset of the accumulated benefit obligation (ABO), which includes both vested and unvested benefits. Wrong (A): The VBO uses current salary levels, not projected increases; the PBO incorporates projected salary growth. Wrong (B): The VBO is typically less than or equal to both the ABO and PBO, not larger. Wrong (D): The VBO is a pension concept specific to defined benefit plans; defined contribution plans have no benefit obligation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3400;

-- Q3404: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Add $200,000 to increase the change in net position","Add $800,000 representing the full pension contribution amount","Subtract $200,000 because contributions exceeded accrual-basis expense","Make no adjustment since pension items reconcile automatically"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GASB 68, the fund statements record the $800,000 cash contribution as an expenditure, while government-wide statements recognize only $600,000 pension expense on the accrual basis. The $200,000 excess must be subtracted in reconciliation because fund expenditures overstated the accrual-basis cost. Wrong (A): Adding $200,000 would widen the difference rather than reconcile it; the fund already overstated pension costs. Wrong (B): The full $800,000 is already reflected in fund expenditures and is not an additional reconciling item. Wrong (D): Different measurement bases create a $200,000 reconciling difference that requires explicit adjustment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3404;

-- Q3405: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Reported as a current fund liability on the balance sheet","Excluded because long-term liabilities are omitted from fund statements","Classified as deferred inflows of resources on the fund statements","Disclosed only in the notes to the financial statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB 34, governmental fund balance sheets use the current financial resources measurement focus, which excludes long-term liabilities. Only currently due amounts (matured principal and interest) appear as fund liabilities. Long-term bonds payable are reported on the government-wide statement of net position. Wrong (A): General obligation bonds are long-term and do not meet the criteria for current fund liability recognition. Wrong (C): Deferred inflows represent resources not yet available; long-term debt is a liability, not a deferred inflow. Wrong (D): While notes provide supplemental information, the bonds are fully reported on government-wide statements, not notes-only.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3405;

-- Q3406: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Bond premium amortization also reduces interest expenditures in funds","Bond premium is treated identically across both types of statements","Bond premium is recognized as revenue in each subsequent fiscal year","Bond premium is not amortized and full coupon payments are expenditures"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): In governmental funds, bond premium received at issuance is recorded as an other financing source. There is no subsequent amortization; the full coupon interest payment is recorded as expenditure each period. On government-wide statements, premium is amortized to reduce interest expense, creating a reconciling difference. Wrong (A): Governmental funds do not amortize bond premium; amortization is a government-wide full accrual concept only. Wrong (B): The treatment differs significantly between fund-level modified accrual and government-wide full accrual accounting. Wrong (C): Bond premium is an other financing source at issuance, not revenue, and is not recognized in subsequent periods.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3406;

-- Q3407: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Reported as a pension expenditure in the current fiscal year","Not reported because it represents a long-term liability change","Classified as deferred inflows of resources on fund statements","Recorded as an other financing use in the fund statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB 68, changes in net pension liability are excluded from governmental fund statements because the current financial resources measurement focus does not recognize long-term liabilities. Pension effects appear only on government-wide statements, creating a reconciling difference. Wrong (A): Fund expenditures reflect only cash pension contributions, not changes in the actuarially determined liability. Wrong (C): Deferred inflows related to pensions are reported on government-wide statements, not governmental fund statements. Wrong (D): Other financing uses involve transfers and debt proceeds, not pension liability changes.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3407;

-- Q3411: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A deferred outflow amortized over the shorter of old or new debt life","An immediate loss recognized in the current reporting period","Revenue from the extinguishment of the original bond obligation","A deferred inflow of resources amortized over the new debt life"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under GASB 65, the difference between the reacquisition price and the net carrying amount of the old debt in an advance refunding is reported as a deferred outflow of resources. It is amortized over the shorter of the remaining life of the old debt or the life of the new debt. Wrong (B): GASB 65 requires deferral and systematic amortization rather than immediate recognition of the loss. Wrong (C): A refunding loss is not revenue; it represents additional cost incurred to retire debt early. Wrong (D): A deferred inflow would apply if there were a gain, and amortization uses the shorter of both debt terms, not just the new debt.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3411;

-- Q3412: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Ignored entirely in the reconciliation between the two statements","An adjustment increasing or decreasing the change in net position","Reported as an other financing source or use in fund statements","Classified as a deferred inflow on the government-wide statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB 34, modified accrual funds recognize interest when due, while full accrual government-wide statements recognize interest as it accrues. The $7,000 increase in accrued interest payable means more interest expense was recognized government-wide than was paid, requiring a downward adjustment in reconciliation. Wrong (A): Accrued interest differences between measurement bases must be reconciled, not ignored. Wrong (C): Accrued interest payable changes are not other financing sources or uses; they are accrual-basis adjustments. Wrong (D): Accrued interest payable is a liability, not a deferred inflow of resources.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3412;

-- Q3413: Interfund Transactions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Reported as profit retained by the internal service fund","Eliminated so governmental activities reflect only the actual cost","Reclassified as miscellaneous revenue on government-wide statements","Ignored because internal charges require no consolidation adjustment"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB 34, internal service funds are consolidated with governmental activities on government-wide statements. The $20,000 markup is eliminated because the government cannot profit from transactions with itself, reducing the expense to the actual $130,000 cost. Wrong (A): Internal service fund profits from intragovernmental transactions are eliminated in consolidation, not reported separately. Wrong (C): The overcharge is not revenue; it is an intercompany markup that must be eliminated. Wrong (D): Internal service fund overcharges must be eliminated to prevent overstating governmental activities expenses.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3413;

-- Q3416: Interfund Transactions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Due from capital projects fund reported as a current asset","Advance to capital projects fund with nonspendable fund balance offset","An expenditure of $500,000 charged to the General Fund","An other financing use reducing the General Fund balance"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB 34, long-term interfund loans are reported as "advances to/from" rather than "due to/from." The General Fund must offset the noncurrent advance with a nonspendable fund balance designation, indicating those resources are unavailable for current spending. Wrong (A): "Due from" is reserved for short-term interfund receivables; a 3-year loan requires the "advance" classification. Wrong (C): The loan creates a receivable, not an expenditure; the General Fund expects repayment. Wrong (D): Advances are not financing transactions; they are lending arrangements between funds.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3416;

-- Q3418: Risk Management and Economics [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Random and unpredictable in both direction and magnitude","Determined solely by relative inflation rates between countries","Always zero because efficient markets eliminate all rate differentials","Equal to the expected change in the exchange rate between currencies"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Interest rate parity theory holds that the forward exchange rate premium or discount between two currencies equals the interest rate differential. With U.S. rates at 5% and U.K. at 3%, the dollar trades at approximately a 2% forward discount against the pound, preventing risk-free arbitrage. Wrong (A): Interest rate differentials have a systematic, predictable relationship with forward exchange rates under parity theory. Wrong (B): Purchasing power parity links exchange rates to inflation; interest rate parity specifically connects forward rates to nominal rate differentials. Wrong (C): Rate differentials persist across countries; parity theory explains how forward rates offset them, not that they disappear.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3418;

-- Q3421: Stock Compensation and Business Combinations [medium/L2]
