-- Q4841: Business Combinations [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The acquiree restating assets and liabilities to fair value with goodwill recorded","The acquiree continuing to use historical cost in its separate statements","The acquirer recording the acquiree''s assets at historical cost in consolidation","Eliminating the need for any subsequent consolidation adjusting entries"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 805-50, push-down accounting requires the acquiree to adjust its standalone financial statements to reflect the acquirer''s basis. Assets and liabilities are restated to acquisition-date fair value, and any goodwill or bargain purchase gain is recorded on the acquiree''s own books. Wrong (B): Push-down accounting specifically replaces historical cost with the acquirer''s fair value basis on the acquiree''s books. Wrong (C): Push-down affects the acquiree''s separate statements, not the acquirer''s consolidated recording basis. Wrong (D): Consolidation entries may still be required for intercompany transactions even when push-down is applied.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4841;

-- Q4848: Foreign Currency Transactions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Apply the current rate method and report CTA adjustments in OCI","Discontinue consolidation entirely until the inflation rate subsides","Translate using purchasing power parity rates adjusted for inflation","Apply the temporal method as if the parent''s currency were functional"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 830-10-45-11, when a foreign subsidiary operates in a highly inflationary economy (cumulative inflation exceeding 100% over three years), its statements are remeasured using the temporal method as if the parent''s reporting currency were the functional currency. Remeasurement gains and losses flow through income. Wrong (A): The current rate method applies to stable-currency subsidiaries; highly inflationary economies require the temporal method. Wrong (B): ASC 830 does not permit discontinuing consolidation due to inflation; remeasurement is required instead. Wrong (C): Purchasing power parity rates are an economic theory concept, not an accepted translation method under ASC 830.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4848;

-- Q4857: Foreign Currency Transactions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Net income in the current reporting period as incurred","Other comprehensive income until the hedged transaction affects earnings","Goodwill as part of the hedged asset''s carrying amount","A contra-asset account netted against the hedged inventory item"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 815-30-35-3, the effective portion of gain or loss on a cash flow hedge is reported in other comprehensive income (OCI) and reclassified into earnings when the hedged forecasted transaction affects earnings. Any ineffective portion is recognized in current earnings immediately. Wrong (A): Recording effective amounts in current income is the treatment for fair value hedges, not cash flow hedges. Wrong (C): Hedge gains and losses have no relationship to goodwill under any hedge accounting model in ASC 815. Wrong (D): Cash flow hedge amounts are deferred in AOCI, not netted against the hedged item''s carrying amount.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4857;

-- Q4858: Foreign Currency Transactions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Written off directly against retained earnings in the disposal period","Left permanently in accumulated other comprehensive income (AOCI)","Reclassified from AOCI into net income as part of disposal gain or loss","Reversed against the carrying amount of the investment account"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 830-30-40-1, upon sale or substantially complete liquidation of a foreign entity, the cumulative translation adjustment in AOCI is reclassified into net income as part of the gain or loss on disposal. This releases previously deferred exchange rate effects into earnings. Wrong (A): The CTA is reclassified through net income via the disposal gain/loss, not written off directly against retained earnings. Wrong (B): AOCI balances related to a disposed foreign entity must be released upon disposal, not retained permanently. Wrong (D): The CTA is reclassified into the income statement, not reversed against the investment''s carrying amount.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4858;

-- Q4874: Intangible Assets Advanced [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No change because the useful life classification is fixed at acquisition","Immediately write off the full trademark carrying amount as impaired","Reclassify as finite-lived and retroactively amortize from acquisition date","Reclassify as finite-lived and begin prospective amortization over five years"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 350-30-35-16 through 35-18, when an indefinite-lived intangible is determined to have a finite useful life, the entity tests for impairment and then begins amortizing prospectively over the estimated remaining life. This change in estimate is applied prospectively under ASC 250. Wrong (A): ASC 350 requires reassessment of useful life each period; the classification is not permanently locked at acquisition. Wrong (B): Immediate write-off is inappropriate unless the asset has no remaining value; here the trademark has five years of useful life. Wrong (C): Retroactive amortization is incorrect; ASC 250 treats useful life changes as prospective adjustments, not retrospective restatements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4874;

-- Q4879: Intangible Assets Advanced [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An impairment loss of $500,000 equal to the fair value excess","No impairment because the fair value exceeds the carrying amount","An impairment loss of $2,000,000 equal to the full goodwill balance","Goodwill reduced to $500,000 reflecting the fair value cushion"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 350-20-35-3, goodwill impairment exists only when a reporting unit''s carrying amount exceeds its fair value. Here, the fair value of $7,500,000 exceeds the carrying amount of $7,000,000, so no impairment loss is recognized. Wrong (A): The $500,000 represents excess fair value over carrying amount (a cushion), not an impairment loss. Wrong (C): Writing off all goodwill is required only when fair value falls below carrying amount minus goodwill, which is not the case here. Wrong (D): Goodwill is not adjusted to reflect the fair value cushion; it remains at $2,000,000 with no impairment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4879;

-- Q4880: Intangible Assets Advanced [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Write off the full $4,000,000 as an impairment loss upon abandonment","Continue reporting at $4,000,000 as an indefinite-lived intangible asset","Reclassify the entire $4,000,000 carrying amount to goodwill","Amortize the $4,000,000 over a default ten-year estimated useful life"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 350-30-35-19, when an acquired IPR&D project is abandoned, the full carrying amount is written off as an impairment loss in the period of abandonment. Since IPR&D is not amortized while in process, the original $4,000,000 fair value remains as the amount to be written off. Wrong (B): An abandoned project has no future economic benefit and cannot continue to be reported as an intangible asset. Wrong (C): Abandoned IPR&D is not reclassified to goodwill; it is written off through the income statement. Wrong (D): Amortization applies only when an IPR&D project is successfully completed, not when it is abandoned.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4880;
